{
  "message": "**Question:** How should the service respond when `tier` is missing, null, or not a string, as well as when it names an unsupported tier?\nWhy it matters: A single explicit rule lets callers and automated tests rely on consistent validation behavior without risking changes to rate-limit state.\n\n**Recommended:** Option A — Return HTTP 400 with `{\"error\":\"tier must be one of: free, pro, enterprise\"}` for every missing, null, non-string, or unsupported `tier`. This is consistent with the existing unknown-tier contract and avoids inventing extra error variants.\n\n| Option | Description |\n|--------|-------------|\n| A | Use the same HTTP 400 response and exact error body above for all missing, null, non-string, or unsupported `tier` values. |\n| B | Keep the current exact body for unsupported tiers, but define distinct error bodies for missing, null, and non-string values. |\n| C | Reject a missing `tier` with a required-field error and use the current exact body for null, non-string, and unsupported values. |\n| Short | Provide a different policy (<=5 words). |\n\nYou can reply with the option letter (e.g., “A”), accept the recommendation by saying “yes” or “recommended”, or provide your own short answer.",
  "requestedSchema": {
    "properties": {
      "decision": {
        "type": "string",
        "title": "Tier validation policy",
        "oneOf": [
          {
            "const": "A",
            "title": "A — same error for every invalid tier (recommended)"
          },
          {
            "const": "B",
            "title": "B — distinct error bodies"
          },
          {
            "const": "C",
            "title": "C — missing tier gets its own error"
          }
        ],
        "default": "A",
        "description": "You may also provide a different policy in 5 words or fewer."
      }
    }
  }
}
